1
00:00:25,959 --> 00:00:28,193
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,336
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:51,483
♪ ♪

4
00:01:18,479 --> 00:01:22,414
♪ ♪

5
00:01:48,041 --> 00:01:51,976
♪ ♪

6
00:02:26,813 --> 00:02:28,946
Mr. Barnett, you
stated that you left work

7
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:31,816
at the Bedford Country
Club after 1:00 a.m.

8
00:02:31,851 --> 00:02:35,287
Did you see anything in
the country club parking lot?

9
00:02:35,322 --> 00:02:37,267
Well, as I was
walking to my car,

10
00:02:37,291 --> 00:02:40,392
I saw Mr. Walsh's car.

11
00:02:40,427 --> 00:02:42,172
It looked like there
was some difficulty,

12
00:02:42,196 --> 00:02:43,862
so I went over.

13
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:48,534
He was lying on the ground,
bleeding from his head.

14
00:02:48,569 --> 00:02:50,468
He needed help.

15
00:02:50,504 --> 00:02:53,405
I knew there was a
phone at the guard gate.

16
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:54,818
But then I spotted another car

17
00:02:54,842 --> 00:02:56,620
and it looked like there
was someone inside,

18
00:02:56,644 --> 00:02:57,976
so I ran to it.

19
00:02:58,011 --> 00:03:00,612
It was Drew Carey.

20
00:03:00,647 --> 00:03:02,781
Drew Carey the defendant?

21
00:03:02,816 --> 00:03:05,417
Yes. He seemed to be passed out.

22
00:03:05,452 --> 00:03:08,720
And there was a
lug wrench in his lap.

23
00:03:08,756 --> 00:03:11,423
It was covered with blood.

24
00:03:11,458 --> 00:03:16,128
Mr. Walsh and Mr. Carey
had argued violently earlier,

25
00:03:16,163 --> 00:03:19,131
but I never thought
Mr. Carey would murder him.

26
00:03:19,166 --> 00:03:20,565
Objection.

27
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:22,134
This witness's thoughts

28
00:03:22,169 --> 00:03:23,446
are irrelevant,
unfounded and conjecture.

29
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:24,847
I didn't kill Uncle Edwin!

30
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:26,672
Mr. Carey. Sit down, Drew.

31
00:03:26,707 --> 00:03:28,652
I didn't kill him,
but I know who did.

32
00:03:28,676 --> 00:03:30,486
If someone would
put up my bail, I...

33
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:31,854
Mr. Matlock,
control your client.

34
00:03:31,878 --> 00:03:33,345
Yes, ma'am.

35
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,247
I swear to you, I'll
tape your mouth shut.

36
00:03:35,282 --> 00:03:37,382
Sorry, Your Honor.

37
00:03:37,418 --> 00:03:40,052
This fight you mentioned,

38
00:03:40,087 --> 00:03:43,155
Mr. Barnett, did
you actually see it

39
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:44,689
or did you just hear about it?

40
00:03:44,724 --> 00:03:47,025
Happened right in front of me.

41
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:49,394
<i>Mr. Walsh, Drew's uncle,</i>

42
00:03:49,429 --> 00:03:52,697
<i>was sitting at the
bar with Mr. Horton.</i>

43
00:03:52,732 --> 00:03:55,833
<i>He's the chief executive
officer of Horton Aviation</i>

44
00:03:55,869 --> 00:03:57,068
<i>where Mr. Walsh worked.</i>

45
00:03:57,104 --> 00:03:58,303
Ed, please.

46
00:03:58,338 --> 00:04:00,806
Ah, thank you.

47
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:03,219
We've had this
conversation before.

48
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:04,977
I don't want to have it again.

49
00:04:05,012 --> 00:04:07,312
But this is trouble,
Jonathan, big trouble.

50
00:04:07,348 --> 00:04:10,315
For you, for me,
for the company.

51
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:12,717
Edwin, you worry too much.

52
00:04:17,724 --> 00:04:20,136
I'd like to go home
now. I'm exhausted.

53
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:22,560
Well go ahead. I
can't go just now.

54
00:04:22,596 --> 00:04:24,396
You're not coming?

55
00:04:24,431 --> 00:04:26,297
I'm auditing the club books.

56
00:04:26,333 --> 00:04:28,033
I'll be home after 1:00.

57
00:04:28,068 --> 00:04:29,701
I won't wait up.

58
00:04:32,706 --> 00:04:34,506
Oh, hey, hey, Valerie.

59
00:04:34,541 --> 00:04:36,240
Where's your fiancée?

60
00:04:36,276 --> 00:04:39,377
Out looking for a rich husband.

61
00:04:39,413 --> 00:04:42,814
Aw, she broke your engagement?

62
00:04:42,849 --> 00:04:45,409
Couldn't happen
to a nicer guy, right?

63
00:04:54,094 --> 00:04:55,171
Hey, Martin.

64
00:04:55,195 --> 00:04:56,661
Double tequila straight up.

65
00:04:56,696 --> 00:04:58,763
How you doing, Uncle Edwin?

66
00:04:58,798 --> 00:04:59,864
I've been looking for you.

67
00:04:59,899 --> 00:05:01,139
Don't serve him, Martin.

68
00:05:01,168 --> 00:05:04,236
Don't serve me?

69
00:05:04,271 --> 00:05:06,938
It's time you stopped
feeling so sorry for yourself.

70
00:05:06,973 --> 00:05:08,884
Uncle Edwin, I
don't want a pep talk.

71
00:05:08,908 --> 00:05:11,187
I just lost Caroline,
my money...

72
00:05:11,211 --> 00:05:13,078
Nobody stole the money from you.

73
00:05:13,113 --> 00:05:14,245
You blew it.

74
00:05:14,281 --> 00:05:16,159
$2 million, your
entire inheritance

75
00:05:16,183 --> 00:05:19,151
on some fly-by-night
software company.

76
00:05:19,186 --> 00:05:20,485
I-I don't need a lecture.

77
00:05:20,521 --> 00:05:21,753
I need a loan.

78
00:05:21,788 --> 00:05:24,122
I've offered you a job, Drew.

79
00:05:24,157 --> 00:05:27,459
A lousy entry-level
job at a lousy salary.

80
00:05:27,494 --> 00:05:29,227
I started that way.

81
00:05:29,262 --> 00:05:32,197
So did your father.

82
00:05:37,003 --> 00:05:38,915
Look, you can loan me the money.

83
00:05:38,939 --> 00:05:40,872
You know you can.

84
00:05:41,975 --> 00:05:44,342
I won't do it.

85
00:05:44,377 --> 00:05:46,244
Don't ask me again.

86
00:05:46,279 --> 00:05:49,014
If I had half, you
know, maybe Caroline...

87
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:50,882
She's a cash register.
Good riddance.

88
00:05:50,917 --> 00:05:51,997
Good riddance?!

89
00:05:53,686 --> 00:05:55,720
You're making
a fool of yourself.

90
00:05:55,755 --> 00:05:58,856
You're a big man, Uncle Edwin.

91
00:05:58,892 --> 00:06:00,058
But just wait.

92
00:06:01,661 --> 00:06:03,328
Everybody falls.

93
00:06:03,363 --> 00:06:05,663
I did, and you will, too!

94
00:06:10,103 --> 00:06:12,070
Then Drew stormed out.

95
00:06:12,105 --> 00:06:14,072
So Mr. Carey
threatened his uncle?

96
00:06:14,107 --> 00:06:17,142
Your Honor, that doesn't
sound like a threat to me.

97
00:06:17,177 --> 00:06:19,522
I just told him
off! I was upset!

98
00:06:19,546 --> 00:06:20,656
Mr. Carey, you are out of order.
- I never said...

99
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:22,547
Drew, sit down!

100
00:06:22,582 --> 00:06:23,615
I'm sorry, Your Honor.

101
00:06:23,650 --> 00:06:25,183
I didn't kill him!

102
00:06:25,218 --> 00:06:28,458
Your Honor, may I have
a moment with my client?

103
00:06:28,482 --> 00:06:29,454
- Explain gag.

104
00:06:29,489 --> 00:06:31,123
Explain restraint.

105
00:06:32,726 --> 00:06:34,503
This court is in
recess for 15 minutes.

106
00:06:35,996 --> 00:06:38,107
What the hell's the
matter with you?!

107
00:06:38,131 --> 00:06:41,132
They're burying me and
you're just sitting there!

108
00:06:41,168 --> 00:06:43,901
How many times have you
ever been in a courtroom?

109
00:06:43,937 --> 00:06:45,636
Twice.

110
00:06:45,672 --> 00:06:47,238
Twice.

111
00:06:47,274 --> 00:06:49,652
I've spent more
years in a courtroom

112
00:06:49,676 --> 00:06:51,977
than you've been alive.

113
00:06:52,012 --> 00:06:55,380
I've listened in courtrooms,
I've spoken in courtrooms,

114
00:06:55,415 --> 00:06:57,215
I've won in courtrooms.

115
00:06:57,250 --> 00:06:59,951
But there's one thing I've
never done in a courtroom,

116
00:06:59,987 --> 00:07:01,586
and that's just sit there.

117
00:07:01,621 --> 00:07:02,921
Okay.

118
00:07:02,956 --> 00:07:04,934
Hey, but we both know
Valerie killed Uncle Edwin.

119
00:07:04,958 --> 00:07:06,391
She'll be on the stand next.

120
00:07:06,426 --> 00:07:08,004
Yeah, but she'll lie. We'll see.

121
00:07:08,028 --> 00:07:09,272
But... No buts,

122
00:07:09,296 --> 00:07:12,531
no ifs, no ands, no
maybes. You got that?

123
00:07:12,566 --> 00:07:14,933
You were a nice kid.

124
00:07:14,968 --> 00:07:16,868
Good-natured, responsible.

125
00:07:16,904 --> 00:07:19,537
How did you turn
out to be such a jerk?

126
00:07:27,548 --> 00:07:30,059
Drew did nothing
but argue with Edwin.

127
00:07:30,083 --> 00:07:32,551
So the argument they
had that night at the club

128
00:07:32,586 --> 00:07:34,385
wasn't their first?

129
00:07:34,421 --> 00:07:35,887
No.

130
00:07:35,923 --> 00:07:37,255
Drew wanted a loan.

131
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:38,868
He wanted one for a long time.

132
00:07:38,892 --> 00:07:41,392
Edwin refused, of course,

133
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:43,595
and then Drew gave him no peace.

134
00:07:43,630 --> 00:07:45,341
He followed him everywhere.

135
00:07:45,365 --> 00:07:46,609
He confronted him.

136
00:07:46,633 --> 00:07:48,545
He blamed him. Don't
even think about it.

137
00:07:48,569 --> 00:07:49,779
For what, I don't know.

138
00:07:49,803 --> 00:07:52,737
Were these
arguments ever violent?

139
00:07:52,772 --> 00:07:54,539
They came close.

140
00:07:54,574 --> 00:07:57,041
Edwin was losing patience.

141
00:07:57,076 --> 00:07:59,210
He referred to
their confrontations

142
00:07:59,246 --> 00:08:01,546
as "Drew's temper tantrums."

143
00:08:01,581 --> 00:08:03,414
That's a lie! Hey.

144
00:08:03,449 --> 00:08:04,494
Whatever our differences,

145
00:08:04,518 --> 00:08:05,650
he respected me.

146
00:08:05,685 --> 00:08:07,685
He offered me a job that night.

147
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:10,166
You think I'd kill somebody
that offered me a job?

148
00:08:10,190 --> 00:08:11,656
Young man, sit down.

149
00:08:11,691 --> 00:08:12,857
I can't take this anymore.

150
00:08:12,892 --> 00:08:14,292
No, I can't handle this.

151
00:08:14,327 --> 00:08:15,693
Bailiff, stop him.

152
00:08:15,728 --> 00:08:17,373
You got to go back to your seat.

153
00:08:17,397 --> 00:08:19,798
Hey, hey, come back here!

154
00:08:19,833 --> 00:08:21,811
Looks as though
we will be adding

155
00:08:21,835 --> 00:08:24,068
escape and battery
on a peace officer

156
00:08:24,103 --> 00:08:27,271
to the charges against
your client, Mr. Matlock.

157
00:08:27,306 --> 00:08:29,507
This court is now in recess.

158
00:08:48,662 --> 00:08:50,528
You go that way.

159
00:09:00,506 --> 00:09:02,073
Hey, stop! Carey!

160
00:09:02,108 --> 00:09:04,943
Carey, stop! Stop!

161
00:09:27,366 --> 00:09:28,934
Hey, sorry, it won't start.

162
00:10:06,973 --> 00:10:08,840
You couldn't call?

163
00:10:08,875 --> 00:10:11,910
Les, I've had a
very difficult day.

164
00:10:11,945 --> 00:10:14,913
You could have called.

165
00:10:14,948 --> 00:10:19,617
Les, I said you could eat here

166
00:10:19,652 --> 00:10:21,986
while they're working
on your kitchen.

167
00:10:22,022 --> 00:10:25,656
Nobody said anything
about cooking for me.

168
00:10:25,692 --> 00:10:28,059
You could have called.

169
00:10:28,095 --> 00:10:31,662
It's, it's not like we're
married or anything.

170
00:10:31,698 --> 00:10:33,264
We're not even going together.

171
00:10:33,299 --> 00:10:34,510
Heaven forbid.

172
00:10:34,534 --> 00:10:37,102
But you could have called.

173
00:10:37,137 --> 00:10:41,306
Les, I've had a
very difficult day.

174
00:10:41,341 --> 00:10:43,586
Yeah, I know, I heard
about it on the radio.

175
00:10:43,610 --> 00:10:46,478
Desperate criminal
escapes courthouse.

176
00:10:46,513 --> 00:10:48,157
Boy, you pick some
real winners, don't you?

177
00:10:48,181 --> 00:10:49,481
He didn't do it.

178
00:10:49,516 --> 00:10:50,881
Of course he did.

179
00:10:50,917 --> 00:10:52,450
No, he didn't.

180
00:10:52,486 --> 00:10:54,564
Then why else would he run away?

181
00:10:54,588 --> 00:10:57,888
Because he doesn't have
the sense God gave a goldfish.

182
00:10:57,924 --> 00:11:00,658
Oh, dumb, too, huh?

183
00:11:00,693 --> 00:11:05,196
Les, if your mother
left you $2 million,

184
00:11:05,231 --> 00:11:07,132
would you grow up to be a jerk?

185
00:11:07,167 --> 00:11:09,434
She did, and I'm
a lovely person.

186
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:16,007
What is this?

187
00:11:16,042 --> 00:11:19,110
Ham hocks and beans.

188
00:11:19,145 --> 00:11:20,578
Where'd you get the ham hocks?

189
00:11:20,613 --> 00:11:22,491
What do you mean,
where'd I get the ham hocks?

190
00:11:22,515 --> 00:11:23,848
Out of your refrigerator.

191
00:11:23,883 --> 00:11:26,584
There were no ham
hocks in the refrigerator.

192
00:11:31,825 --> 00:11:36,294
Well... I guess I'll go
see if I can find him.

193
00:11:36,329 --> 00:11:37,962
Where're you going?

194
00:11:37,997 --> 00:11:39,497
See if I can find him. I...

195
00:11:39,532 --> 00:11:41,766
Oh, aren't you gonna eat first?

196
00:11:41,801 --> 00:11:43,167
I don't want any.

197
00:11:43,202 --> 00:11:44,947
You don't want any?!

198
00:11:44,971 --> 00:11:46,682
I've been cooking all day!

199
00:11:46,706 --> 00:11:49,541
You can at least
try a bite or two.

200
00:11:50,577 --> 00:11:51,976
You're hot stuff, you know that?

201
00:11:52,011 --> 00:11:54,256
I mean, I've been
cooking all day!

202
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:59,050
I missed<i> Oprah, Phil Donahue,
Joan Rivers</i> and<i> Dr. Ruth.</i>

203
00:11:59,085 --> 00:12:01,485
I don't know what's going on!

204
00:12:02,756 --> 00:12:04,756
Hey, you could've called!

205
00:12:29,315 --> 00:12:30,793
You killed him, Valerie.

206
00:12:30,817 --> 00:12:32,083
Get out of my house.

207
00:12:32,118 --> 00:12:34,129
Not until you tell
me you killed him.

208
00:12:34,153 --> 00:12:36,688
I didn't kill Edwin;
he was my husband.

209
00:12:36,723 --> 00:12:39,557
What about the Riverview Motel?

210
00:12:39,593 --> 00:12:41,125
I don't know what
you're talking about.

211
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:44,395
And that guy you
were with... Aunt Val.

212
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:45,597
Does he know how much

213
00:12:45,632 --> 00:12:47,264
you loved Uncle Edwin?

214
00:12:48,568 --> 00:12:49,868
I saw you with him.

215
00:12:49,903 --> 00:12:51,603
In a restaurant.

216
00:12:51,638 --> 00:12:54,572
I followed you, Aunt Val.

217
00:12:54,607 --> 00:12:57,075
You little creep.

218
00:12:57,110 --> 00:12:59,488
Uncle Edwin found out
about your affair, didn't he?

219
00:12:59,512 --> 00:13:01,257
And then he threatened
to divorce you.

220
00:13:01,281 --> 00:13:03,514
You don't want that.

221
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:06,484
Your boyfriend, he's got
muscles but he hasn't got dough.

222
00:13:06,519 --> 00:13:08,197
I didn't kill Edwin. Now leave!

223
00:13:08,221 --> 00:13:10,555
You didn't go home the night
Uncle Edwin was murdered.

224
00:13:10,590 --> 00:13:11,700
You waited outside the club

225
00:13:11,724 --> 00:13:13,669
and hit him on the head
with his own wrench!

226
00:13:13,693 --> 00:13:15,627
No!

227
00:13:17,697 --> 00:13:19,430
All right, I didn't come home.

228
00:13:19,466 --> 00:13:20,831
I was at the Riverview Motel.

229
00:13:21,968 --> 00:13:23,279
Hello? Anybody home?

230
00:13:23,303 --> 00:13:24,303
In here!

231
00:13:27,707 --> 00:13:29,140
Oh, it figures.

232
00:13:29,176 --> 00:13:30,541
Let go of her.

233
00:13:35,448 --> 00:13:37,793
This is Valerie Walsh
at 1245 Sky Way.

234
00:13:37,817 --> 00:13:40,377
Please send the
police immediate...

235
00:13:40,687 --> 00:13:42,998
Drew!

236
00:13:43,022 --> 00:13:45,300
Give her back her keys and
sit down and wait for the police.

237
00:13:45,324 --> 00:13:46,891
What are you, nuts?

238
00:13:48,661 --> 00:13:50,027
Hey!

239
00:13:52,131 --> 00:13:53,864
Hey!

240
00:13:58,271 --> 00:13:59,271
Drew!

241
00:14:15,354 --> 00:14:16,999
You see where he was headed?

242
00:14:17,023 --> 00:14:18,056
No.

243
00:14:18,091 --> 00:14:19,869
Any idea where he was headed?

244
00:14:19,893 --> 00:14:20,858
Uh, no.

245
00:14:20,894 --> 00:14:22,827
O-Officer, I'm his lawyer.

246
00:14:22,862 --> 00:14:25,041
Mr. Matlock, are
you gonna stick to

247
00:14:25,065 --> 00:14:27,899
this client-lawyer privileged
information business?

248
00:14:27,934 --> 00:14:29,411
This man could be a murderer.

249
00:14:29,435 --> 00:14:31,335
Oh, I don't think so.

250
00:14:31,370 --> 00:14:33,638
He-he's pretty
much of a jackass,

251
00:14:33,673 --> 00:14:36,474
but I don't think
he'd kill anybody.

252
00:14:36,509 --> 00:14:39,549
Thanks for your
help. Yeah, right.

253
00:14:41,781 --> 00:14:43,147
Uh, Mrs. Walsh...

254
00:14:43,182 --> 00:14:45,549
I have a lover; his
name is Mark Tannen.

255
00:14:45,585 --> 00:14:49,553
At the time of the murder, we
were at the Riverview Motel.

256
00:14:49,589 --> 00:14:52,389
Boy, you come right
out with it, don't you?

257
00:14:53,927 --> 00:14:56,961
Uh, wh-why didn't, uh,
you mention your lover

258
00:14:56,997 --> 00:14:58,295
the first time we met?

259
00:14:58,331 --> 00:15:01,699
My affair has nothing to
do with Edwin's murder.

260
00:15:01,735 --> 00:15:04,535
Mark is a good man and
he loves me and I love him.

261
00:15:04,570 --> 00:15:07,639
Uh, is... got a phone
number on-on hand?

262
00:15:07,674 --> 00:15:09,974
555-9737.

263
00:15:10,010 --> 00:15:11,542
But he's out of town.

264
00:15:11,577 --> 00:15:13,878
Uh, uh, uh... seven.

265
00:15:13,913 --> 00:15:16,481
Uh, did, uh, did-did Mr. Horton

266
00:15:16,516 --> 00:15:18,616
and your husband, uh, get along?

267
00:15:18,651 --> 00:15:21,152
I mean, were they, were
they friends, business friends?

268
00:15:21,187 --> 00:15:22,153
They argued.

269
00:15:22,188 --> 00:15:23,154
They did?

270
00:15:23,189 --> 00:15:24,155
Constantly.

271
00:15:24,190 --> 00:15:25,356
Here, at the club...

272
00:15:25,391 --> 00:15:26,658
Club? That night?

273
00:15:27,961 --> 00:15:28,961
About what?

274
00:15:28,995 --> 00:15:30,294
Well, I couldn't really

275
00:15:30,330 --> 00:15:32,474
hear them, but I know
they were definitely arguing.

276
00:15:32,498 --> 00:15:33,731
And here?

277
00:15:33,766 --> 00:15:35,566
Behind closed doors.

278
00:15:35,602 --> 00:15:38,269
Probably something having
to do with Horton Aviation.

279
00:15:38,304 --> 00:15:41,405
Edwin was president
and Jonathan was CEO.

280
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:43,441
Edwin kept his business from me.

281
00:15:43,476 --> 00:15:48,146
Of course, you kept
certain things from him.

282
00:15:48,181 --> 00:15:50,881
That's not funny.

283
00:15:52,652 --> 00:15:55,119
Um, wh-why didn't you mention

284
00:15:55,155 --> 00:15:57,956
th-their arguments before?

285
00:15:57,991 --> 00:15:59,735
I thought that
Drew had killed him.

286
00:15:59,759 --> 00:16:01,092
Oh, you thought Drew...

287
00:16:01,127 --> 00:16:02,226
Mm.

288
00:16:02,261 --> 00:16:05,897
And, uh, nobody had accused you.

289
00:16:05,932 --> 00:16:08,465
That, too.

290
00:16:08,501 --> 00:16:10,768
Good-bye, Mr. Matlock.

291
00:16:11,871 --> 00:16:14,038
Oh, uh...

292
00:16:16,709 --> 00:16:18,887
We're tied four to four
in the seventh inning.

293
00:16:18,911 --> 00:16:21,979
We have runners at first
and second with only one out.

294
00:16:22,014 --> 00:16:25,783
And Al Warner with that 95
mile-an-hour fastball of his

295
00:16:25,818 --> 00:16:27,718
is challenging McCord.

296
00:16:27,753 --> 00:16:30,321
Excuse me.

297
00:16:30,356 --> 00:16:33,591
35 for a double, 40 for cable.

298
00:16:33,626 --> 00:16:35,025
List of movies is over there.

299
00:16:35,061 --> 00:16:36,027
Yeah, well...

300
00:16:36,062 --> 00:16:38,729
uh, what I really want

301
00:16:38,764 --> 00:16:40,797
is a list of people
that stayed here

302
00:16:40,833 --> 00:16:42,400
on the night of September 12.

303
00:16:42,435 --> 00:16:45,303
You got to be a cop and
you got to have a warrant.

304
00:16:45,338 --> 00:16:46,937
Oh.

305
00:16:46,972 --> 00:16:49,173
Oh, darn, you know,

306
00:16:49,208 --> 00:16:51,408
all I've got is one of these.

307
00:16:57,183 --> 00:16:59,850
Actually, I've got two.

308
00:17:07,894 --> 00:17:09,438
Hey, rather than ruin it,

309
00:17:09,462 --> 00:17:11,874
why don't I just
take the whole thing.

310
00:17:11,898 --> 00:17:13,797
Okay.

311
00:17:15,902 --> 00:17:19,436
Mr. Matlock, if I don't get
to the airport in 15 minutes,

312
00:17:19,472 --> 00:17:22,039
I'm gonna miss my
plane for Washington,

313
00:17:22,075 --> 00:17:24,153
and that means I'll miss my
appointment at the Pentagon.

314
00:17:24,177 --> 00:17:25,543
Uh, what,

315
00:17:25,578 --> 00:17:28,546
what were you and Edwin
Walsh arguing about that night?

316
00:17:28,581 --> 00:17:30,948
Who said we were arguing?

317
00:17:30,983 --> 00:17:32,517
Valerie Walsh.

318
00:17:32,552 --> 00:17:34,018
What the hell does she know?

319
00:17:34,054 --> 00:17:35,820
No, Edwin and I
were just talking.

320
00:17:35,855 --> 00:17:37,421
About what?

321
00:17:37,456 --> 00:17:38,755
Can't say.

322
00:17:38,791 --> 00:17:39,991
Why not?

323
00:17:40,026 --> 00:17:43,361
We work with the
Defense Department.

324
00:17:43,396 --> 00:17:45,329
Oh, classified.

325
00:17:45,364 --> 00:17:46,697
That's right, that's right.

326
00:17:46,732 --> 00:17:48,243
Does that answer your
question, Mr. Matlock?

327
00:17:48,267 --> 00:17:49,311
Oh, of course, of course.

328
00:17:49,335 --> 00:17:50,434
Now, if you'll excuse me,

329
00:17:50,469 --> 00:17:52,181
I've got to make a
phone call. Yes, sir.

330
00:17:52,205 --> 00:17:54,972
Mr. Walsh's office
is-is on this floor?

331
00:17:55,007 --> 00:17:56,239
Two doors down to the right.

332
00:17:56,275 --> 00:17:57,575
Oh, thank you.

333
00:18:07,420 --> 00:18:09,887
Well, where'd everything go?

334
00:18:09,922 --> 00:18:11,889
Storage mostly.

335
00:18:11,924 --> 00:18:14,759
They cleaned this room
out the day after the murder.

336
00:18:14,794 --> 00:18:17,495
Not that there was ever
much in here to begin with.

337
00:18:17,530 --> 00:18:20,297
Mr. Walsh didn't
believe in frills.

338
00:18:20,332 --> 00:18:24,535
He spent company money like
it came out of his own pocket.

339
00:18:24,570 --> 00:18:27,505
Well, is it standard
procedure around here

340
00:18:27,540 --> 00:18:30,875
to put a person's
stuff in storage

341
00:18:30,910 --> 00:18:34,311
before his body even gets cold?

342
00:18:34,346 --> 00:18:37,314
All I know is, Mr. Horton
gave the order.

343
00:18:37,349 --> 00:18:40,250
And when he says "jump,"
we don't ask why, we just...

344
00:18:40,286 --> 00:18:41,452
Say, "How high?"

345
00:18:41,487 --> 00:18:43,287
Yep. Oh, yeah.

346
00:18:44,991 --> 00:18:46,835
Mark, it's ready.

347
00:18:46,859 --> 00:18:48,792
All right.

348
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:01,350
Mark Tannen, right?

349
00:19:01,374 --> 00:19:02,384
Drew Carey.

350
00:19:02,408 --> 00:19:03,908
Valerie's nephew.

351
00:19:03,943 --> 00:19:06,210
I don't know any Valerie.

352
00:19:06,245 --> 00:19:08,345
And get off my truck.

353
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:09,381
Hey, look, look!

354
00:19:09,415 --> 00:19:11,615
Buddy, I know all
about you and Aunt Val,

355
00:19:11,650 --> 00:19:14,952
so you can cooperate or
the police will know, too.

356
00:19:14,988 --> 00:19:16,587
What do you want?

357
00:19:16,622 --> 00:19:19,990
Where were you the night
her husband was murdered?

358
00:19:21,761 --> 00:19:23,194
I was with her.

359
00:19:23,229 --> 00:19:25,163
Yeah, uh, where?

360
00:19:25,198 --> 00:19:26,797
Riverview Motel.

361
00:19:26,832 --> 00:19:30,401
Oh, then how come your name
wasn't in the motel registry?

362
00:19:30,436 --> 00:19:31,536
Says who?

363
00:19:31,571 --> 00:19:33,270
Well, I have it right here.

364
00:19:33,305 --> 00:19:35,450
And you know damn
well your name isn't in it.

365
00:19:35,474 --> 00:19:37,274
Now, come on, Mark.

366
00:19:37,309 --> 00:19:39,043
Did she murder Edwin or did you?

367
00:19:41,547 --> 00:19:42,914
Ow!

368
00:19:56,395 --> 00:19:58,028
Who are you?

369
00:19:59,799 --> 00:20:01,364
Where'd you come from?

370
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:02,800
You're the killer!

371
00:20:02,835 --> 00:20:04,202
Uh, Ben's not here!

372
00:20:04,237 --> 00:20:06,571
I'll wait.

373
00:20:06,606 --> 00:20:09,774
Uh, he-he went to Buffalo
to a lawyers convention.

374
00:20:09,809 --> 00:20:11,275
He won't be back for weeks.

375
00:20:11,310 --> 00:20:14,544
Uh, this is not a good hideout.

376
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:17,314
Yeah, uh, policemen
are in and out of here

377
00:20:17,349 --> 00:20:19,194
all the time, you know.

378
00:20:19,218 --> 00:20:21,096
Policemen just love Ben.

379
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:23,287
And Ben's friends.

380
00:20:23,322 --> 00:20:25,623
Th-They drop in
every ten seconds or so

381
00:20:25,658 --> 00:20:27,792
to see me and just
to see how I'm doing.

382
00:20:27,827 --> 00:20:29,371
One's due here any second now.

383
00:20:29,395 --> 00:20:31,640
Would you just stop talking
for a minute there, fella?

384
00:20:31,664 --> 00:20:32,630
Ben!

385
00:20:32,665 --> 00:20:34,732
Ixnay, the killernay.

386
00:20:34,767 --> 00:20:35,799
What?

387
00:20:35,835 --> 00:20:37,735
Oh, hi, Ben. Look...

388
00:20:39,138 --> 00:20:40,104
Call the police!

389
00:20:40,139 --> 00:20:41,205
You can't do that!

390
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:42,506
Don't-don't give me

391
00:20:42,541 --> 00:20:43,741
that-that lawyer-client

392
00:20:43,776 --> 00:20:45,676
relationship garbage!
I'm off the case!

393
00:20:45,712 --> 00:20:47,055
Give me the keys. You can't!

394
00:20:47,079 --> 00:20:48,890
Mark Tannen's the killer.
I saw him today. No, no.

395
00:20:48,914 --> 00:20:50,425
He lied to me. I'm going
back there tomorrow.

396
00:20:50,449 --> 00:20:51,627
Give me the keys to the car.

397
00:20:51,651 --> 00:20:53,095
I have to find out who
killed Uncle Edwin!

398
00:20:53,119 --> 00:20:54,851
No! Give me the keys!

399
00:20:54,887 --> 00:20:57,955
You've made me look like
the worst kind of stupid lawyer

400
00:20:57,990 --> 00:21:00,023
who can't even
control his own client.

401
00:21:00,059 --> 00:21:02,626
You're spoiled, you're
arrogant, you're stupid.

402
00:21:02,661 --> 00:21:03,827
Give me the keys!

403
00:21:03,862 --> 00:21:04,828
Ben, let me explain...

404
00:21:04,863 --> 00:21:05,963
Shut up and sit down!

405
00:21:05,998 --> 00:21:07,098
Now, listen, Ben...

406
00:21:07,133 --> 00:21:08,165
Ben said sit down!

407
00:21:35,161 --> 00:21:37,828
You're wearing my best suit!

408
00:21:37,863 --> 00:21:39,363
Well, your friend

409
00:21:39,398 --> 00:21:42,667
threw flour on mine,
and this is all I could find.

410
00:21:42,702 --> 00:21:46,270
Be careful with it.

411
00:21:46,305 --> 00:21:47,972
I see you cleaned
up the kitchen.

412
00:21:48,007 --> 00:21:50,485
Yeah, I-I can't eat breakfast
in the middle of a mess.

413
00:21:50,509 --> 00:21:52,009
You didn't get anything on...

414
00:21:52,045 --> 00:21:53,377
No, no, no, no.

415
00:21:53,412 --> 00:21:56,047
Here, know what, just sit
down and-and eat, okay?

416
00:21:56,082 --> 00:21:57,092
Eat.

417
00:22:01,721 --> 00:22:03,154
Oh! Yeah.

418
00:22:03,189 --> 00:22:04,155
Breakfast.

419
00:22:04,190 --> 00:22:06,090
Oh, boy!

420
00:22:06,125 --> 00:22:09,994
I love... breakfast.

421
00:22:10,029 --> 00:22:13,664
Yeah. Mmm! Mm-mm-mm-mm-mm-mm!

422
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:15,066
Mmm...

423
00:22:15,101 --> 00:22:17,868
A little something to get
you started with. Yeah.

424
00:22:17,904 --> 00:22:19,904
Mmm...

425
00:22:19,939 --> 00:22:21,605
Yeah.

426
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:25,409
I'm glad you got done
before Les gets over here.

427
00:22:25,444 --> 00:22:27,110
Who is he? Neighbor.

428
00:22:29,215 --> 00:22:30,514
Retired, plastics.

429
00:22:30,550 --> 00:22:32,528
When something
breaks down in his house,

430
00:22:32,552 --> 00:22:34,218
he hangs around over here.

431
00:22:34,253 --> 00:22:35,686
Weird dude.

432
00:22:35,721 --> 00:22:36,931
Yeah.

433
00:22:38,391 --> 00:22:42,359
'Course I'm a little weird
myself, too, sometimes,

434
00:22:42,395 --> 00:22:44,261
so we make a pretty good team.

435
00:22:45,598 --> 00:22:47,998
Drew...

436
00:22:50,002 --> 00:22:51,902
I've been thinking about you.

437
00:22:53,439 --> 00:22:56,273
You've been unhappy for
a long time, haven't you?

438
00:22:56,308 --> 00:22:58,242
I... I can understand now.

439
00:22:58,277 --> 00:22:59,944
I mean, you're...

440
00:22:59,979 --> 00:23:01,411
wanted for murder,

441
00:23:01,447 --> 00:23:03,092
and your mother
left you $2 million,

442
00:23:03,116 --> 00:23:04,548
and you blew that.

443
00:23:04,583 --> 00:23:07,918
And in one roll of
the dice... Gone.

444
00:23:07,953 --> 00:23:11,555
Somewhere up there, uh...

445
00:23:11,590 --> 00:23:16,693
my father's laughing at me,
saying, "Drew, you're no good.

446
00:23:16,728 --> 00:23:19,563
Never were."

447
00:23:19,598 --> 00:23:23,500
Well, your father
was a hard man.

448
00:23:23,536 --> 00:23:26,103
Hard on himself,
hard on your mother.

449
00:23:26,138 --> 00:23:29,206
He... he tolerated me
'cause I was his lawyer.

450
00:23:29,241 --> 00:23:31,609
He was real hard on you.

451
00:23:31,644 --> 00:23:35,212
My father didn't like me.

452
00:23:35,247 --> 00:23:40,517
Well, your father
didn't like anybody.

453
00:23:40,552 --> 00:23:45,589
But you were his son, so...

454
00:23:45,624 --> 00:23:47,391
Well, I... I liked you.

455
00:23:47,426 --> 00:23:50,060
Yeah?

456
00:23:50,096 --> 00:23:51,395
Yeah.

457
00:23:51,430 --> 00:23:54,298
Yeah, yeah, you were...
you were a good kid.

458
00:23:54,333 --> 00:23:55,699
You got good marks in school,

459
00:23:55,734 --> 00:23:57,968
and you had a nice personality

460
00:23:58,004 --> 00:23:59,670
and I... I just liked you.

461
00:24:03,976 --> 00:24:05,175
I'll go see who it is.

462
00:24:05,210 --> 00:24:06,410
Stay right here.

463
00:24:06,445 --> 00:24:09,580
Stay out of sight.

464
00:24:20,392 --> 00:24:23,627
Why, Mr. Horton,
what a surprise.

465
00:24:23,662 --> 00:24:27,030
Well, my plane just
got in from Washington,

466
00:24:27,065 --> 00:24:29,065
and, uh, I took the
liberty of dropping by.

467
00:24:29,101 --> 00:24:30,267
Hope you don't mind.

468
00:24:30,302 --> 00:24:31,669
How come?

469
00:24:31,704 --> 00:24:33,081
Well, I was under
the gun yesterday,

470
00:24:33,105 --> 00:24:36,006
and I was rather short with
you; that was rude of me and I...

471
00:24:36,042 --> 00:24:37,308
I want to apologize.

472
00:24:37,343 --> 00:24:40,110
Oh, there's no
apologies necessary.

473
00:24:40,145 --> 00:24:43,347
Classified information
is classified information.

474
00:24:43,382 --> 00:24:45,182
Well, it's no longer classified.

475
00:24:45,218 --> 00:24:46,616
You see, Horton Aviation

476
00:24:46,652 --> 00:24:49,086
is building a new
military supply transport.

477
00:24:49,121 --> 00:24:51,221
And it's going to be
unveiled in about two weeks,

478
00:24:51,256 --> 00:24:53,457
so it's no longer top secret.

479
00:24:53,492 --> 00:24:54,836
And now you know
the... the subject

480
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:56,193
of my discussion with Edwin.

481
00:24:56,228 --> 00:24:59,396
Oh, that's what you and
Edwin were arguing about...

482
00:24:59,431 --> 00:25:01,899
The military supply
transport. All right.

483
00:25:01,934 --> 00:25:03,467
Argument is, well...

484
00:25:03,502 --> 00:25:04,568
rather a strong word.

485
00:25:04,603 --> 00:25:07,071
We were discussing
design details.

486
00:25:07,106 --> 00:25:09,540
You know, the cant of the
wing, sectors of the hull.

487
00:25:09,575 --> 00:25:11,575
There are thousands
of those details.

488
00:25:11,610 --> 00:25:12,776
It's all very complicated,

489
00:25:12,811 --> 00:25:14,278
but you get the idea?

490
00:25:14,313 --> 00:25:16,280
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

491
00:25:16,315 --> 00:25:19,683
Yeah. And it was, it was
nice of you to-to stop by.

492
00:25:19,718 --> 00:25:21,852
Oh, look at that car.

493
00:25:21,887 --> 00:25:25,456
Now, that's some car!

494
00:25:25,491 --> 00:25:26,790
Well...

495
00:25:26,826 --> 00:25:28,870
Does the leather still
have that leather smell?

496
00:25:31,029 --> 00:25:35,031
Mmm! Well, some of these days.

497
00:25:35,067 --> 00:25:37,935
Uh, Edwin drove a car
a lot like this, didn't he?

498
00:25:37,970 --> 00:25:39,213
Exactly the same. Oh.

499
00:25:39,237 --> 00:25:40,949
All of my executives drive them.

500
00:25:40,973 --> 00:25:42,918
We get the fleet price. Oh.

501
00:25:42,942 --> 00:25:44,708
Oh. You get many flats?

502
00:25:44,743 --> 00:25:47,444
Never.

503
00:25:47,479 --> 00:25:49,179
Never had one.

504
00:25:49,214 --> 00:25:50,925
Well, I guess Edwin
was just unlucky

505
00:25:50,949 --> 00:25:52,917
all the way around that night.

506
00:25:52,952 --> 00:25:54,763
If he had called someone
to change that flat,

507
00:25:54,787 --> 00:25:56,520
he'd probably be alive today.

508
00:25:56,555 --> 00:25:57,688
But that's Edwin.

509
00:25:57,723 --> 00:26:00,024
Counting pennies till the end.

510
00:26:00,059 --> 00:26:02,026
Getting along without him

511
00:26:02,061 --> 00:26:04,395
is going to be just
about impossible. Well...

512
00:26:04,430 --> 00:26:06,597
thanks. See you someday.

513
00:26:06,632 --> 00:26:08,766
Okay.

514
00:26:08,801 --> 00:26:10,467
Mmm...

515
00:26:10,502 --> 00:26:13,670
Well, take care.

516
00:26:30,489 --> 00:26:32,422
Drew, I told you to stay put.

517
00:26:32,457 --> 00:26:33,690
What if he had seen you?

518
00:26:33,726 --> 00:26:34,803
I'd have taken off.

519
00:26:34,827 --> 00:26:35,971
Sooner or later,
you're going to have

520
00:26:35,995 --> 00:26:37,528
to give yourself up to the cops.

521
00:26:37,563 --> 00:26:39,963
You keep taking off, you're
going to wind up dead.

522
00:26:39,999 --> 00:26:41,510
Yeah. I got to get back
to that construction site.

523
00:26:41,534 --> 00:26:42,699
No, no, no, no.

524
00:26:42,735 --> 00:26:45,002
I'll go see what I can unravel.

525
00:26:45,037 --> 00:26:46,481
Oh, but if you
just let me help...

526
00:26:46,505 --> 00:26:49,406
No! We're both in
over our heads already.

527
00:26:50,743 --> 00:26:53,043
Here. Put this on.

528
00:26:53,078 --> 00:26:54,222
Oh, but I can't...

529
00:26:54,246 --> 00:26:57,047
Take off my good suit,
put that on, and stay here.

530
00:27:12,664 --> 00:27:14,831
Ben, I couldn't
sleep last night,

531
00:27:14,866 --> 00:27:17,426
thinking of you alone
here with that killer.

532
00:27:19,572 --> 00:27:22,006
You're wearing Ben's best suit.

533
00:27:22,041 --> 00:27:23,674
You got a car?

534
00:27:23,709 --> 00:27:25,676
Sure. It's parked
in my driveway.

535
00:27:34,453 --> 00:27:36,453
Hey, Ben. Uh...

536
00:27:36,488 --> 00:27:38,288
Looking for your client?

537
00:27:38,323 --> 00:27:41,302
I don't know, Bob. Want one?

538
00:27:41,326 --> 00:27:43,393
He actually decked
the bailiff, huh?

539
00:27:43,428 --> 00:27:45,896
That's the rumor.

540
00:27:45,931 --> 00:27:47,175
Any clues on him?

541
00:27:47,199 --> 00:27:48,465
Nah, not a peep.

542
00:27:48,500 --> 00:27:50,367
I'll bet you could
give us a hand.

543
00:27:50,402 --> 00:27:52,614
Yeah. He's out at my house.

544
00:27:52,638 --> 00:27:53,815
Why don't you take a look?

545
00:27:55,474 --> 00:27:57,775
So, what is it with you
and this kid, huh, Ben?

546
00:27:57,810 --> 00:27:59,209
Why don't you drop him?

547
00:27:59,245 --> 00:28:01,111
Can't do that.

548
00:28:01,147 --> 00:28:03,046
I'm sure the court
will let you withdraw.

549
00:28:03,082 --> 00:28:06,116
Well, more than that.

550
00:28:06,152 --> 00:28:09,219
I know that when you
look at Drew Carey,

551
00:28:09,255 --> 00:28:11,733
you're looking at a jerk.

552
00:28:11,757 --> 00:28:13,190
Well, you are, too.

553
00:28:13,225 --> 00:28:15,525
Well, yeah.

554
00:28:15,560 --> 00:28:18,896
But I knew him when
he was a little kid.

555
00:28:18,931 --> 00:28:21,376
You know? He used to
deliver papers and work.

556
00:28:21,400 --> 00:28:23,044
He was a nice,
honorable little fella.

557
00:28:23,068 --> 00:28:24,545
I know what happened to him.

558
00:28:24,569 --> 00:28:25,981
I know what happened to him,

559
00:28:26,005 --> 00:28:28,049
and I... I guess
that's the difference.

560
00:28:28,073 --> 00:28:30,540
And, uh, besides that,

561
00:28:30,575 --> 00:28:32,720
I don't think he did it.

562
00:28:32,744 --> 00:28:34,711
It's a good case.

563
00:28:34,746 --> 00:28:37,981
Yours? No. I don't think so.

564
00:28:38,017 --> 00:28:42,686
Um, I got to go in and
see Judge Cooksey.

565
00:28:44,623 --> 00:28:47,757
He gets mad when anybody
comes in his office eating,

566
00:28:47,793 --> 00:28:49,726
so...

567
00:28:52,631 --> 00:28:55,699
Uh, Ben, you hear from
that boy, you let us know.

568
00:28:55,734 --> 00:28:57,367
Not a chance!

569
00:29:01,073 --> 00:29:02,739
Look, Mr. Matlock,

570
00:29:02,774 --> 00:29:05,175
I registered under a false name.

571
00:29:05,210 --> 00:29:07,244
That's why it's not there.

572
00:29:07,279 --> 00:29:09,212
What name did you use?

573
00:29:12,151 --> 00:29:14,662
You weren't at the
motel, were you?

574
00:29:14,686 --> 00:29:16,453
Why would I lie?

575
00:29:16,488 --> 00:29:18,655
Well, that's for you to tell me.

576
00:29:18,690 --> 00:29:20,924
Or the police.

577
00:29:20,959 --> 00:29:22,825
And, uh, if I were you,

578
00:29:22,861 --> 00:29:25,128
I'd tell me because

579
00:29:25,164 --> 00:29:28,831
the police a lot of times
don't believe ex-cons.

580
00:29:31,570 --> 00:29:33,137
Do you know?

581
00:29:33,172 --> 00:29:34,505
Oh, yeah.

582
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:36,473
Okay.

583
00:29:38,076 --> 00:29:40,978
I was at a racetrack outside
of Butler Township in Alabama.

584
00:29:41,013 --> 00:29:42,212
By yourself?

585
00:29:42,247 --> 00:29:44,547
A bunch of guys.

586
00:29:44,583 --> 00:29:47,617
Just so you know,
Valerie told me to lie.

587
00:29:49,221 --> 00:29:51,889
Valerie told you to say
you were at that motel?

588
00:29:51,924 --> 00:29:54,191
She called me
yesterday after that run-in

589
00:29:54,226 --> 00:29:55,525
with you and her nephew.

590
00:29:55,560 --> 00:29:58,295
She doesn't know
where I was that night.

591
00:29:58,330 --> 00:30:00,264
You see, we, um...

592
00:30:00,299 --> 00:30:01,398
had some girls with us.

593
00:30:01,433 --> 00:30:04,368
Mostly for luck. Mr. Matlock?

594
00:30:06,271 --> 00:30:09,872
That night was the only
time I ever broke parole.

595
00:30:09,908 --> 00:30:11,775
Are you going to say anything?

596
00:30:12,878 --> 00:30:15,078
Not unless I have to.

597
00:30:15,113 --> 00:30:16,446
Thanks.

598
00:30:16,481 --> 00:30:18,415
Thanks.

599
00:30:26,458 --> 00:30:28,436
You know what this
means now, don't you?

600
00:30:28,460 --> 00:30:30,905
Hey, what the hell?!

601
00:30:30,929 --> 00:30:32,006
Damn it, Drew.

602
00:30:32,030 --> 00:30:33,730
Now you've stolen Les's car.

603
00:30:33,765 --> 00:30:36,366
Somebody get him!

604
00:30:36,401 --> 00:30:37,641
I'm tired of dealing with you!

605
00:30:42,374 --> 00:30:44,274
Hello?

606
00:30:44,309 --> 00:30:47,944
Ms. Walsh? Uh,
this is Ben Matlock,

607
00:30:47,980 --> 00:30:49,346
Drew Carey's lawyer.

608
00:30:49,381 --> 00:30:50,613
Yes?

609
00:30:50,649 --> 00:30:53,317
I-I only have a
minute to talk to you.

610
00:30:53,352 --> 00:30:56,453
Uh, Drew didn't
kill your husband.

611
00:30:56,488 --> 00:30:59,155
I-I-I don't, I don't
know who did,

612
00:30:59,191 --> 00:31:01,291
but I think we can end

613
00:31:01,327 --> 00:31:04,227
one of our more
immediate problems.

614
00:31:04,262 --> 00:31:07,397
Uh, Drew is on his
way over to see you.

615
00:31:07,432 --> 00:31:09,366
He is? I'm calling the police.

616
00:31:09,401 --> 00:31:13,003
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

617
00:31:13,038 --> 00:31:15,205
Yeah, that's right.
Call the cops.

618
00:31:33,825 --> 00:31:36,359
Valerie, open up!

619
00:31:36,395 --> 00:31:37,461
Valerie.

620
00:31:37,496 --> 00:31:39,996
Valerie, open up!

621
00:31:40,031 --> 00:31:41,965
Valerie!

622
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:43,867
Valerie, open the door!

623
00:31:43,902 --> 00:31:46,280
Valerie!

624
00:31:46,304 --> 00:31:47,937
Valerie, open up!

625
00:31:47,973 --> 00:31:49,206
What do you want?

626
00:31:49,241 --> 00:31:52,442
Mark Tannen just
blew your alibi.

627
00:31:52,477 --> 00:31:54,522
He wasn't with you
that night at the motel.

628
00:31:54,546 --> 00:31:56,491
He was at the racetrack.

629
00:31:56,515 --> 00:31:57,948
Yeah.

630
00:31:57,983 --> 00:32:00,717
A bunch of guys and
women can vouch for him.

631
00:32:01,887 --> 00:32:03,186
What?

632
00:32:03,221 --> 00:32:05,656
You killed Uncle
Edwin, didn't you?

633
00:32:05,691 --> 00:32:07,323
No, I was covering for Mark.

634
00:32:07,359 --> 00:32:08,792
I thought Mark had killed him.

635
00:32:11,797 --> 00:32:13,362
You killed Uncle Edwin!

636
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:17,567
I was at Harry's Bar
from 10:00 till 1:00.

637
00:32:17,602 --> 00:32:18,835
Check with the bartender.

638
00:33:29,775 --> 00:33:32,576
Do you have to do
that at the dinner table?

639
00:33:32,611 --> 00:33:36,279
You don't have to look.

640
00:33:36,314 --> 00:33:39,916
Well, you got the picture of
the dead body right on the bread.

641
00:33:41,186 --> 00:33:43,252
Well, there.

642
00:33:49,094 --> 00:33:50,994
When's the trial?

643
00:33:51,029 --> 00:33:52,162
Starts tomorrow.

644
00:33:53,865 --> 00:33:57,567
You must have some idea
by now who the real killer is.

645
00:33:57,603 --> 00:33:58,802
Well...

646
00:33:58,837 --> 00:34:00,670
It's not Valerie.

647
00:34:00,705 --> 00:34:02,239
It's not her boyfriend.

648
00:34:02,274 --> 00:34:04,441
How do you know? Their alibis.

649
00:34:04,476 --> 00:34:06,476
It's not Drew.

650
00:34:06,511 --> 00:34:08,178
Oh, sure.

651
00:34:08,213 --> 00:34:10,747
It's not cute, little,
sweet, little Drew.

652
00:34:10,782 --> 00:34:11,982
Well, it's not.

653
00:34:12,017 --> 00:34:13,183
He stole my car.

654
00:34:13,218 --> 00:34:14,350
Stealing a car is

655
00:34:14,386 --> 00:34:16,219
a lot different from murder.

656
00:34:16,254 --> 00:34:17,487
Well, then who's left?

657
00:34:17,522 --> 00:34:18,855
Jonathan Horton.

658
00:34:18,890 --> 00:34:20,256
Who's he?

659
00:34:20,292 --> 00:34:22,459
He's the victim's boss.

660
00:34:22,494 --> 00:34:24,105
It's him. It's got to be him.

661
00:34:24,129 --> 00:34:26,296
Is it just because
you don't like him

662
00:34:26,331 --> 00:34:27,564
or do you have proof?

663
00:34:29,668 --> 00:34:32,035
I don't like him.

664
00:34:32,070 --> 00:34:34,170
I don't have any proof.

665
00:34:37,709 --> 00:34:39,843
Oh.

666
00:34:41,646 --> 00:34:42,913
Hmm.

667
00:34:44,149 --> 00:34:45,848
Hmm...

668
00:34:45,884 --> 00:34:48,818
Seems like the
wheel of a nice car.

669
00:34:50,289 --> 00:34:51,988
It is the wheel of a nice car.

670
00:34:52,024 --> 00:34:55,058
All the executives in that
company drive cars like that.

671
00:34:55,093 --> 00:34:57,094
Oh, nice company. Yeah.

672
00:34:57,129 --> 00:35:00,797
They buy a lot of the same
model of the same car,

673
00:35:00,832 --> 00:35:02,832
and then they get a
big break on the price.

674
00:35:11,142 --> 00:35:12,409
You know what?

675
00:35:12,444 --> 00:35:14,778
What?

676
00:35:14,813 --> 00:35:17,247
Mr. Walsh's car...

677
00:35:17,282 --> 00:35:19,983
is not like Mr. Horton's car.

678
00:35:20,018 --> 00:35:21,751
Really?

679
00:35:21,786 --> 00:35:23,720
Yeah.

680
00:35:26,625 --> 00:35:28,991
Excuse me. I got to
make a phone call.

681
00:35:29,026 --> 00:35:30,860
Thanks for dinner.

682
00:35:30,895 --> 00:35:32,395
Great lamb. Excuse me.

683
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:34,898
If was beef stroganoff.

684
00:35:39,604 --> 00:35:41,971
Hmm...

685
00:35:42,007 --> 00:35:44,574
Mr. Horton,

686
00:35:44,609 --> 00:35:47,510
how would you
characterize the decedent?

687
00:35:47,546 --> 00:35:51,448
Was-was he
dependable, hardworking?

688
00:35:51,483 --> 00:35:54,451
He was an asset
to Horton Aviation.

689
00:35:54,486 --> 00:35:56,719
Extremely competent.

690
00:35:56,755 --> 00:35:58,488
Loyal, fair.

691
00:35:58,523 --> 00:36:00,690
Honest? To a fault.

692
00:36:00,725 --> 00:36:02,425
Did you ever argue with him?

693
00:36:02,460 --> 00:36:07,997
Well, we-we had some
pretty good discussions, but...

694
00:36:08,033 --> 00:36:10,433
And very loudly, perhaps,

695
00:36:10,468 --> 00:36:13,737
but I wouldn't say
that we... we argued.

696
00:36:13,772 --> 00:36:16,572
So you had, you had discussions?

697
00:36:16,608 --> 00:36:21,778
Discussions about the
military supply transport

698
00:36:21,813 --> 00:36:23,813
that you'd just built?

699
00:36:23,849 --> 00:36:25,548
Yes.

700
00:36:25,583 --> 00:36:29,152
And the prototype alone
cost the government

701
00:36:29,187 --> 00:36:33,389
nearly $1 billion, didn't it?

702
00:36:33,424 --> 00:36:34,758
Yes.

703
00:36:34,793 --> 00:36:36,325
Yeah, hmm, phew!

704
00:36:36,361 --> 00:36:37,594
Hmm. What's it called?

705
00:36:37,629 --> 00:36:40,296
The Horton MST.

706
00:36:40,331 --> 00:36:42,465
It's a very complex machine.

707
00:36:42,500 --> 00:36:44,167
Horton MST.

708
00:36:44,202 --> 00:36:50,674
Is it expensive
because it's complex,

709
00:36:50,709 --> 00:36:55,145
or because you
padded expenditures?

710
00:36:55,180 --> 00:36:57,247
Like-like say for instance,

711
00:36:57,282 --> 00:37:01,251
$50 instead of 50
cents for a bolt?

712
00:37:01,286 --> 00:37:03,764
That's a serious
charge, Mr. Matlock...

713
00:37:03,788 --> 00:37:05,522
And false.

714
00:37:05,557 --> 00:37:09,191
You ordered
Mr. Walsh's files sealed

715
00:37:09,227 --> 00:37:12,162
less than 24 hours
after his death. Why?

716
00:37:12,197 --> 00:37:14,197
For security reasons.
Or was it because

717
00:37:14,232 --> 00:37:18,702
he discovered that the
budget for the transport

718
00:37:18,737 --> 00:37:22,739
had been padded?

719
00:37:22,774 --> 00:37:24,708
No. Absolutely not.

720
00:37:24,743 --> 00:37:28,912
I think he had, and
was collecting proof.

721
00:37:28,947 --> 00:37:32,148
Mr. Walsh was a very honest man.

722
00:37:32,184 --> 00:37:35,284
And he would sooner
see Horton Aviation

723
00:37:35,320 --> 00:37:37,821
go down the tubes than
cheat the government.

724
00:37:37,856 --> 00:37:41,791
And that is why you
were arguing with him

725
00:37:41,827 --> 00:37:44,594
and why you took his files

726
00:37:44,629 --> 00:37:47,597
and why you
killed him, isn't it?

727
00:37:47,632 --> 00:37:52,302
This is outrageous,
insulting and false!

728
00:37:52,337 --> 00:37:56,206
Lawyers, including
you, Mr. Matlock,

729
00:37:56,241 --> 00:37:58,375
can be sued for defamation.

730
00:37:58,410 --> 00:38:01,511
The truth is not defamation.

731
00:38:01,546 --> 00:38:05,114
And the truth is, as soon as
the General Accounting Office

732
00:38:05,149 --> 00:38:06,750
goes over your budget,

733
00:38:06,785 --> 00:38:09,652
they will find that you
defrauded the government

734
00:38:09,688 --> 00:38:14,157
to the tune of $11 million
to $12 million, won't they?

735
00:38:14,192 --> 00:38:15,569
Your Honor, this
line of questioning

736
00:38:15,593 --> 00:38:17,071
has nothing to
do with this case.

737
00:38:17,095 --> 00:38:18,595
Goes to motive, Your Honor.

738
00:38:18,630 --> 00:38:20,297
Overruled.

739
00:38:20,332 --> 00:38:22,064
Thank you.

740
00:38:22,100 --> 00:38:27,370
Mr. Horton, uh, all the
executives in your company

741
00:38:27,406 --> 00:38:30,540
drive the same make and
model of car, don't they?

742
00:38:30,575 --> 00:38:32,509
Yes. And you drove

743
00:38:32,544 --> 00:38:35,311
such a car to court
today, didn't you?

744
00:38:35,346 --> 00:38:36,312
Yes.

745
00:38:36,347 --> 00:38:38,514
Ha. Your car.

746
00:38:38,549 --> 00:38:41,584
Let's see, let's see,
let's see, let's see...

747
00:38:41,619 --> 00:38:47,891
Yeah, the license plate
number is KFP848?

748
00:38:47,926 --> 00:38:53,630
And you parked in the
pay parking lot space 93?

749
00:38:53,665 --> 00:38:55,465
I believe so. Mm-hmm.

750
00:38:55,500 --> 00:38:58,768
Would you mind if I borrowed
your keys for a few minutes?

751
00:39:00,905 --> 00:39:02,405
What for?

752
00:39:02,440 --> 00:39:05,341
With the court's permission...

753
00:39:05,377 --> 00:39:11,214
Your car will prove who
the killer of Edwin Walsh is.

754
00:39:22,594 --> 00:39:24,627
Thank you. Yeah.

755
00:39:24,663 --> 00:39:27,296
You mind if the bailiff
takes a little spin?

756
00:39:32,003 --> 00:39:34,403
Okay, George, back 'er right up.

757
00:39:34,439 --> 00:39:36,138
Back 'er right up,

758
00:39:36,174 --> 00:39:38,052
right up the side
of Mr. Walsh's car.

759
00:39:38,076 --> 00:39:39,709
Okay, here, bring 'er to me.

760
00:39:39,744 --> 00:39:41,522
Bring 'er to me,
bring 'er to me.

761
00:39:41,546 --> 00:39:43,112
Hold!

762
00:39:43,147 --> 00:39:44,413
Put 'er right there.

763
00:39:44,449 --> 00:39:46,182
Good, George. Good, George.

764
00:39:49,721 --> 00:39:51,320
All set, Your Honor.

765
00:39:51,356 --> 00:39:53,490
Your Honor, I'd like
to repeat the objection

766
00:39:53,525 --> 00:39:55,992
I raised in chambers
regarding Mr. Matlock's

767
00:39:56,027 --> 00:39:57,827
dog and pony show.

768
00:39:57,862 --> 00:39:59,261
So noted,

769
00:39:59,297 --> 00:40:01,397
but for the moment, overruled.

770
00:40:01,433 --> 00:40:02,932
Proceed, Mr. Matlock.

771
00:40:02,967 --> 00:40:06,436
Now, you've already
given us your version

772
00:40:06,471 --> 00:40:11,174
of what happened the night
that Mr. Walsh was murdered,

773
00:40:11,209 --> 00:40:13,877
but that's not really
what happened, is it?

774
00:40:13,912 --> 00:40:15,845
Of course it's what happened.

775
00:40:15,881 --> 00:40:17,180
Oh, no, no, no.

776
00:40:17,215 --> 00:40:21,985
You left the club, oh,
about an hour after my client.

777
00:40:23,922 --> 00:40:25,288
Good night.

778
00:40:28,826 --> 00:40:31,895
<i>On your way across
the parking lot,</i>

779
00:40:31,930 --> 00:40:35,031
<i>you passed Edwin Walsh's car.</i>

780
00:40:35,066 --> 00:40:38,701
<i>It was empty because
he was still inside the club</i>

781
00:40:38,736 --> 00:40:40,836
<i>auditing the books.</i>

782
00:40:48,413 --> 00:40:51,414
<i>Then you saw Drew's car,</i>

783
00:40:51,449 --> 00:40:56,852
<i>and inside, you saw Drew
passed out in the front seat.</i>

784
00:40:56,888 --> 00:40:58,687
<i>Dead to the world.</i>

785
00:40:58,723 --> 00:41:01,257
<i>And that's when it hit you.</i>

786
00:41:01,292 --> 00:41:03,493
<i>A way of keeping Edwin Walsh</i>

787
00:41:03,528 --> 00:41:05,895
<i>from ever blowing
the whistle on you.</i>

788
00:41:05,930 --> 00:41:09,799
<i>First thing you did was give
Edwin Walsh's car a flat tire.</i>

789
00:41:16,174 --> 00:41:19,175
Then you got in
your car and left,

790
00:41:19,210 --> 00:41:22,811
making sure you said good
night to the security guard

791
00:41:22,847 --> 00:41:24,980
so that he could later testify

792
00:41:25,016 --> 00:41:27,116
that you went home
before the murder.

793
00:41:27,152 --> 00:41:28,618
But you didn't go home.

794
00:41:28,653 --> 00:41:31,253
You parked your car on the road

795
00:41:31,288 --> 00:41:32,822
by the golf course.

796
00:41:32,857 --> 00:41:36,592
<i>Got out, opened your trunk,
took out the lug wrench,</i>

797
00:41:36,628 --> 00:41:38,661
<i>walked back to the parking lot</i>

798
00:41:38,696 --> 00:41:41,430
<i>and waited in the shadows
for Mr. Walsh to come out.</i>

799
00:41:45,469 --> 00:41:48,470
<i>Mr. Walsh saw the flat tire</i>

800
00:41:48,506 --> 00:41:50,673
<i>and started to change it himself</i>

801
00:41:50,708 --> 00:41:52,908
<i>just like you knew he would.</i>

802
00:42:17,702 --> 00:42:20,836
<i>That's when you
hit him over the head</i>

803
00:42:20,872 --> 00:42:23,338
<i>with the lug wrench
from your car.</i>

804
00:42:23,374 --> 00:42:27,377
<i>A blow that killed him.</i>

805
00:42:27,412 --> 00:42:30,480
<i>You then removed the lug
wrench from Mr. Walsh's car,</i>

806
00:42:30,515 --> 00:42:32,582
<i>so two wrenches
wouldn't be found</i>

807
00:42:32,617 --> 00:42:34,917
<i>at the scene of the crime.</i>

808
00:42:34,952 --> 00:42:37,420
<i>Then you placed the
bloodstained murder weapon</i>

809
00:42:37,455 --> 00:42:39,422
<i>in Drew's lap.</i>

810
00:42:39,457 --> 00:42:43,560
<i>Made your way back to
your car and drove away.</i>

811
00:42:43,595 --> 00:42:45,294
How'd I do, Mr. Horton?

812
00:42:45,329 --> 00:42:47,508
Not a single word
of that is true.

813
00:42:47,532 --> 00:42:50,633
Then prove me wrong.

814
00:42:50,669 --> 00:42:54,671
This lug wrench was
found in my client's hand.

815
00:42:54,706 --> 00:42:57,206
Take it...

816
00:42:57,241 --> 00:43:01,944
And remove the lug
nut from the front wheel

817
00:43:01,979 --> 00:43:04,380
of Mr. Walsh's car.

818
00:43:16,127 --> 00:43:18,094
Go ahead, go ahead.

819
00:43:22,333 --> 00:43:26,469
That wrench doesn't seem
to want go on that nut, does it?

820
00:43:26,504 --> 00:43:28,271
Well, it's probably just me.

821
00:43:28,306 --> 00:43:30,807
To be honest, I've
never done this before.

822
00:43:30,842 --> 00:43:31,886
Oh, oh, oh.

823
00:43:31,910 --> 00:43:33,742
Well, just-just for kicks,

824
00:43:33,778 --> 00:43:37,680
how about trying it on
the front wheel of your car.

825
00:43:37,715 --> 00:43:39,248
Just to see.

826
00:43:39,284 --> 00:43:41,284
There. Just to see.

827
00:43:42,620 --> 00:43:44,921
How about that?

828
00:43:44,956 --> 00:43:48,124
How about that? Perfect fit.

829
00:43:48,159 --> 00:43:50,460
See, you assumed

830
00:43:50,495 --> 00:43:53,696
because the cars
are the same model,

831
00:43:53,731 --> 00:43:55,398
the wrenches
would be the same...

832
00:43:55,433 --> 00:43:57,400
Interchangeable.

833
00:43:57,435 --> 00:43:59,902
But they're not.

834
00:43:59,937 --> 00:44:04,339
Because Mr. Walsh
didn't like frills.

835
00:44:04,375 --> 00:44:07,109
He took the wheels that
came from the factory.

836
00:44:07,144 --> 00:44:09,244
But you love frills.

837
00:44:09,280 --> 00:44:12,047
You got all the frills you
could lay your hands on.

838
00:44:12,082 --> 00:44:15,051
Deluxe stereo, tape deck,

839
00:44:15,086 --> 00:44:19,088
special seats, remote
controlled alarm system.

840
00:44:19,123 --> 00:44:23,659
Even fancy wheel covers.

841
00:44:23,694 --> 00:44:29,132
See, this murder weapon
fits the wheels on your car,

842
00:44:29,167 --> 00:44:35,671
because this murder
weapon came out of your trunk.

843
00:44:35,706 --> 00:44:37,039
Didn't it?

844
00:44:42,513 --> 00:44:45,081
We the jury find the
defendant not guilty.

845
00:44:47,851 --> 00:44:49,218
Thanks. There you go.

846
00:44:49,253 --> 00:44:51,287
Order, order.

847
00:44:53,023 --> 00:44:55,858
As for you, Mr. Carey,

848
00:44:55,893 --> 00:44:59,662
you have disrupted this
court, assaulted my bailiff

849
00:44:59,697 --> 00:45:02,164
and escaped from custody.

850
00:45:02,199 --> 00:45:04,633
Now while these crimes
carry jail sentences

851
00:45:04,669 --> 00:45:06,769
of many long years,

852
00:45:06,804 --> 00:45:10,172
in light of the time you
have spent in custody,

853
00:45:10,207 --> 00:45:12,408
awaiting trial on this matter,

854
00:45:12,443 --> 00:45:15,511
and the extreme emotional
tension you were under,

855
00:45:15,546 --> 00:45:20,016
I am recommending
that you be sentenced

856
00:45:20,051 --> 00:45:22,351
to lengthy community service.

857
00:45:24,355 --> 00:45:26,322
This court is adjourned.

858
00:45:41,072 --> 00:45:42,538
Mmm.

859
00:45:44,475 --> 00:45:46,042
Nice bouquet.

860
00:45:48,212 --> 00:45:50,012
$2.75.

861
00:45:50,048 --> 00:45:51,780
A bottle?

862
00:45:51,815 --> 00:45:53,349
$2.75.

863
00:45:53,384 --> 00:45:54,616
No kidding?

864
00:45:54,652 --> 00:45:56,485
We got to keep
this to ourselves.

865
00:45:57,721 --> 00:45:59,088
Well,<i> l'chaim.</i>

866
00:45:59,123 --> 00:46:00,856
<i>Salud.</i>

867
00:46:04,062 --> 00:46:06,295
Mmm.

868
00:46:06,330 --> 00:46:07,597
Mmm.

869
00:46:08,967 --> 00:46:11,144
Well, you were
right... Drew didn't do it.

870
00:46:11,168 --> 00:46:12,668
Well, I told you.

871
00:46:12,703 --> 00:46:17,339
I know, but I thought
right to the last...

872
00:46:17,375 --> 00:46:21,177
You told him you never had a
more contrary, cantankerous,

873
00:46:21,212 --> 00:46:22,978
childish client, didn't you?

874
00:46:23,013 --> 00:46:25,347
Yeah. Yeah.

875
00:46:25,383 --> 00:46:26,882
Well, you got that right. Yeah.

876
00:46:26,917 --> 00:46:28,718
You told him to shape up?

877
00:46:28,753 --> 00:46:32,454
Oh, yeah, and quit trying
that get-rich-quick stuff.

878
00:46:32,489 --> 00:46:33,923
Yeah, and settle down.

879
00:46:33,958 --> 00:46:36,458
Oh, yeah, and be
the reasonable person

880
00:46:36,493 --> 00:46:38,427
that his mother
raised him to be.

881
00:46:40,831 --> 00:46:45,501
Well, that's the way
my mother raised me.

882
00:46:45,536 --> 00:46:50,172
If she said it once, she
said it a million times:

883
00:46:50,208 --> 00:46:54,576
"Lester, I want you to
be a reasonable man.

884
00:46:56,914 --> 00:47:02,784
"Be strong, be firm, be tough,

885
00:47:02,820 --> 00:47:05,154
"but above all...

886
00:47:05,189 --> 00:47:07,857
Be reasonable. be
reasonable." Yeah.

